    41	Verdict: needs-attention
    42	
    43	Block as written. The splice can mistake replay behavior for path mediation, the decision experiment cannot establish irrecoverable information loss, and the optimizer comparison uses an unstable primary endpoint. The participant holdouts and compute budget also need revision.
    44	
    45	Findings:
    46	- [high] The splice can attribute replay artifacts to the original agent (docs/idea-summaries/images/08/path-splicing-factorial.svg:26-50)
    47	  Figure 5 labels the diagonal cells as natural runs, but the pseudocode computes all four cells with fresh report calls and uses intact runs only for the placebo. Mixing intact diagonals with replayed off-diagonals confounds framing with replay. Using four replayed cells instead estimates effects for the replay harness. Even matching both diagonal means cannot rule out a reporter that changes its answer only when a transplanted path conflicts with its referral.
    48	  Recommendation: Use the same stateless report interface in all four cells and redraw the figure. Report the intact-agent total effect separately, define placebo equivalence margins for both referrals, and test off-diagonal conflict with controlled transcripts before extrapolating to intact agents.
    49	- [high] A residual calibration penalty cannot identify lost information (docs/idea-summaries/08-blind-analysis-for-agents.md:114-124)
    50	  The entropy identity concerns the best possible combiner. E5 compares fitted combiners on a small cohort and treats a surviving penalty as evidence of an information bug. A reversible nonlinear shift can defeat a finite-data calibrator. A path change can preserve or add class information, and a direct report can erase it by rounding or clipping. A positive splice contrast plus worse fitted log loss is compatible with all of these cases.
    51	  Recommendation: Keep path mediation and recoverability as separate endpoints. Validate recovery on known invertible and lossy controls that cross both routes. Fit prespecified conditional combiners on development people, check calibration learning curves, and describe an empirical residual as unrecovered by those combiners.
    52	- [high] The shrinkage target is optimal only within a restricted rule (docs/idea-summaries/08-blind-analysis-for-agents.md:165-167)
    53	  The proposed gamma* minimizes squared error over fixed blends of a blind report and a referral class mean. E4 trains unrestricted policies that can use the clip and referral jointly. Even in the stated Gaussian model, the squared-error optimum averages over the class posterior conditional on both the blind measurement and the referral, which generally gives a nonlinear rule. An optimizer can improve accuracy by moving away from the registered gamma band.
    54	  Recommendation: Use gamma* as a prediction for a constrained shrinkage control. For unrestricted agents, compare against a development-fitted conditional predictor and test signed framing effects, MSE, and log loss separately.
    55	- [high] The primary optimizer endpoint fails when framing effects cancel (docs/idea-summaries/08-blind-analysis-for-agents.md:229-235)
    56	  P3 divides the mean indirect effect by the signed total effect. A path effect of +0.2 SD and a report effect of −0.2 SD make the ratio undefined despite substantial effects through both routes. Near cancellation makes the ratio unstable, and opposing effects can put it outside Figure 8's 0-to-1 axis. Figure 8 also draws a defined path share at generation zero alongside zero total effect.
    57	  Recommendation: Make the signed indirect and direct effects in SD units the primary optimizer endpoints and estimate their joint uncertainty. Reserve the ratio for a prespecified minimum absolute total effect and update Figure 8.
    58	- [high] The optimizer comparison cannot isolate what determines the route (docs/idea-summaries/08-blind-analysis-for-agents.md:177-179)
    59	  GEPA and ACE change text and receive reflective feedback, while GRPO changes weights and receives group-normalized scalar feedback. Matching rollout counts leaves those differences bundled with selection rules and update schedules. Both text and weight updates can change tool calls or final numbers. A route difference would compare these three configurations, but it would not establish the mechanism asserted by H5.
    60	  Recommendation: Constrain the claim to the tested configurations, or cross access to reflective feedback with which stage can change. Compare changes from a common starting policy at matched feedback budgets and report each optimizer separately.
    61	- [high] Participant holdouts omit training provenance for reused models (docs/idea-summaries/08-blind-analysis-for-agents.md:183-187)
    62	  StaBLE E1–E3 score the reused pilot LoRA on 151 people, while the proposal establishes only that the 43 pilot holdouts were unseen by that adapter. The other 108 therefore have unverified training exposure. EchoNet has the same gap in a tool at line 239. Its released segmenter used the original training split and selected weights on validation. A fresh downstream patient split can therefore put previously seen people into the sealed replication set. [EchoNet segmentation training code](https://github.com/echonet/dynamic/blob/master/echonet/utils/segmentation.py#L1381-L1517)
    63	  Recommendation: Audit participant manifests for every reused model and tool. Restrict confirmatory LoRA estimates to verified unseen people. Use the original EchoNet test split for sealed scoring, or retrain the segmenter within the new split.
    64	- [high] The referral generator does not guarantee the stated prior (docs/idea-summaries/08-blind-analysis-for-agents.md:84-94)
    65	  Flipping a binary class label with probability q gives a 20% overall error rate at q=0.2. It does not give an 80% probability of being right within each referral unless class prevalence is balanced or the assignment is calibrated by referral. At 15% high-risk prevalence, the high-risk referral has about a 41% chance of being right. If referrals also use age or elite status within fall-risk class, the blind likelihood can depend on referral even before the agent sees it. Either mismatch can produce the log-loss penalty attributed to double counting.
    66	  Recommendation: Specify the assignment matrix and target prevalence, derive the prior from both, and separate the binary theory test from referrals based on multiple covariates. Include a correctly conditioned blind combiner as a control.
    67	- [medium] Referral-dependent exclusions leave the primary outcomes undefined (docs/idea-summaries/08-blind-analysis-for-agents.md:193-209)
    68	  Trial exclusion is an allowed tool choice and a measured framing response, but the splice means, reward, and decision scores all require a numeric report. If the frail referral makes an agent discard difficult clips, complete-case analysis can hide that path effect and make the same arm look more accurate. The preregistered interpretation of a null would then miss a concrete form of steering.
    69	  Recommendation: Define a scored outcome for every assigned clip, including exclusion, repeat requests, and failed reports. Prespecify the decision fallback and report referral-specific coverage alongside numeric effects.
    70	- [medium] The novelty comparison understates the cited intervention studies (docs/idea-summaries/08-blind-analysis-for-agents.md:59-66)
    71	  Xiao and Yu already intervene on search behavior under wrong hypotheses and measure evidence opening and final accuracy. Cuccarese already combines agentic blinding with evolutionary scoring and measures recovery of validated targets. The narrower contribution can survive as a crossed replay intervention on continuous measurements with downstream recalibration. The related-work table currently claims more distance by describing the former as observational with no remedy and omitting the latter's validation. [Xiao and Yu](https://arxiv.org/html/2611.05720v1) · [Cuccarese](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.06013)
    72	  Recommendation: Correct the prior-work descriptions and state the contribution in terms of the specific crossed intervention and downstream recoverability test. Include a fixed-evidence reporting control.
    73	- [medium] The compute budget undercounts the checkpoint splices (docs/idea-summaries/08-blind-analysis-for-agents.md:260-274)
    74	  E4 budgets 12,400 audit calls per loop. At six checkpoints, 129 clips and the stated eight-draw protocol, the four tool-only splice cells already require 24,768 report calls, plus 12,384 natural runs for the two referral arms. The EchoNet splice allocation likewise lists 0.48B tokens for 2,000 videos, while applying the stated three-arm protocol at 15k tokens per call gives 2.88B. Any smaller audit design needs explicit counts and revised power estimates.
    75	  Recommendation: Budget natural runs, placebo and splice calls, and replication by checkpoint, arm, clip, and draw. Measure throughput on one complete checkpoint and size the confirmatory experiment from that benchmark.
    76	
    77	Next steps:
    78	- Freeze the replay interface, signed primary endpoints, referral assignment, and handling of missing reports before opening sealed outcomes.
    79	- Verify participant provenance across all models and tools, then pilot one complete checkpoint to test replay validity, calibration recovery, and compute cost.

